Most laptops don't fail suddenly. They slow down, run hotter, lose battery life and pick up small faults over time — and much of that is preventable. A few minutes of care every month can add years to a laptop's useful life, whether it's brand new or refurbished.

These tips are written with Indian conditions in mind: summer heat, dust, monsoon humidity and unstable power.

## Keep it cool

Heat is the biggest enemy of laptop components and batteries.

### 1. Let it breathe

Laptops pull in air through vents on the bottom or sides. Using a laptop on a bed, pillow or your lap blocks those vents, so the fan works harder and the processor slows itself down to stay safe. Use it on a hard, flat surface — a table, a tray or a simple laptop stand.

### 2. Clean the vents and fan

Dust builds up inside the fan and heatsink, especially in dusty cities. If your laptop is louder or hotter than it used to be, dust is the most likely cause. Gently blow out the vents with a can of compressed air while the laptop is switched off, holding the fan still so it doesn't over-spin. For a thorough internal cleaning — and replacing dried-out thermal paste on older laptops — use a qualified technician.

### 3. Watch where you leave it

Don't leave a laptop in a parked car, on a windowsill in direct sun, or next to a stove. High temperatures age the battery quickly even when the laptop is switched off.

## Look after the battery

### 4. Avoid sitting at 100% all day

Lithium-ion batteries age faster when they are kept at full charge and warm for long periods. If your laptop stays plugged in at a desk, turn on the manufacturer's charge-limit or battery-care setting — most business laptops from Dell, Lenovo and HP offer one, and MacBooks have **Optimised Battery Charging**.

### 5. Don't run it flat regularly

Occasional full discharges are fine, but routinely running the battery to 0% adds wear. Plug in when you get to around 20%.

### 6. Check its health once in a while

Knowing your battery's health helps you plan a replacement before it lets you down. Here's [how to check battery health](/blog/how-to-check-laptop-battery-health) on Windows and macOS.

## Protect it from power problems

### 7. Use a surge protector or UPS

Voltage fluctuations and surges are common in many areas. A good-quality surge protector — or a UPS if power cuts are frequent — protects both the charger and the laptop.

### 8. Use the right charger

Always use the original charger or a good-quality replacement with the correct voltage, connector and wattage. Cheap unbranded chargers can deliver unstable power and are a common cause of charging faults. Unplug the charger by holding the plug, not the cable, and avoid tight bends near the connectors.

## Keep it clean

### 9. Clean the screen and keyboard properly

- Switch off the laptop first.
- Wipe the screen with a dry or very slightly damp microfibre cloth. Never spray liquid directly onto the screen, and avoid ammonia-based glass cleaners.
- Turn the laptop upside down gently and tap it to dislodge crumbs from the keyboard, then use a soft brush or compressed air.
- Isopropyl alcohol on a cloth (not poured) cleans keys and palm rests effectively.

Keep food and drinks away from the keyboard. Even "spill-resistant" keyboards only buy you time to switch off and dry the laptop.

### 10. Mind the monsoon

Humidity can cause corrosion over time. Store the laptop in a dry place, don't carry it in a damp bag, and if it gets wet, switch it off immediately and don't turn it back on until it has been checked and completely dried.

## Keep the software healthy

### 11. Update and declutter

- Install operating system and security updates promptly.
- Update drivers and BIOS/firmware using the manufacturer's official tool.
- Uninstall programs you don't use and limit apps that launch at startup — this alone often makes an older laptop feel faster.
- Keep at least 15–20% of your SSD free so the system has room to work.
- Run the built-in security tool (Windows Security on Windows) and avoid installing software from unofficial sources.

### 12. Back up regularly

Hardware can always fail, and backups are the only real protection for your files. Use a cloud service, an external drive, or both. Follow the simple rule of keeping important files in at least two places.

## Carry it carefully

- Use a padded sleeve or a bag with a laptop compartment.
- Don't pick up an open laptop by its screen — lift it from the base.
- Close the lid gently and make sure nothing (a pen, earphones) is left on the keyboard, which can press into and scratch the screen.

## When to get professional help

Some signs mean it's time for a technician: the fan is loud even when idle, the laptop shuts down on its own, the battery is swollen (the trackpad or base may bulge — stop using it immediately), or the charger or port gets unusually hot. If your laptop is still under warranty, contact the seller first. Here's how [our warranty](/warranty) works.

## Small habits, long life

None of these tips takes more than a few minutes, but together they make a real difference. A well-maintained laptop stays fast and reliable for years — and if you're buying refurbished, it's also the best way to make the most of your purchase.

